was transmitted through professional socialization, fused with a moral commitment that made challenging it professionally costly, and rendered unfalsifiable through three mechanisms: the victim-blaming charge, the complicity charge, and the absorption of alternative explanations. The paper is not an argument that institutional discrimination is a myth. Rather, it’s an argument that sociology no longer treats the question of what causes inequality as open and that has significant consequences."}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "9f914d8e1e35d66d7f2064d8fc3b241c0591c4fb7b61379025135be039b7f228", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "question_abandonment", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.5281/zenodo.19637439", "doi:10.1007/s11186-026-09741-8", "doi:10.1007/s11186-026-09741-8.pdf", "openalex:W7154824760"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19637439", "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11186-026-09741-8", "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11186-026-09741-8.pdf"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}

